EXTRA7 = {
 6: "A rule per coin lets people build conditions such as shared accounts or time delays without changing the protocol. Every full node runs the same interpreter, so the language is kept small on purpose and is easy to implement identically. A language with loops would let one transaction keep every node busy for as long as its author wanted, which is why Script has none.",
 8: "A stack is the simplest memory a language can have: only the top is visible, which is one reason why Script can be checked so fast. Python lists behave in the same way through append and pop, which makes the idea easy to try by hand. Slide 11 collects the vocabulary of this part.",
 11: "The output script and the input script are the two names to keep: one is the lock, the other is the key that opens it, and a node runs them one after the other. The call stack of a Python program is a familiar example of last in, first out: the function called last is the first to return. Two stack words appear in the next locks: OP_DUP copies the top item, and OP_HASH160 replaces it by its hash.",
 12: "In the output of Bitcoin Core and in older books you may still meet the field names scriptSig and scriptPubKey, and matching them with input script and output script saves confusion. Segregated witness moved the signature elsewhere for new outputs, which is one more reason why the neutral names are better.",
 14: "Hashing the key has two effects: the lock is short and of fixed size, and the public key itself stays unseen until the coin is spent. The price is that the spender must supply the key and the signature later, which is what the second box of slide 15 does. Legacy addresses that start with 1 hide exactly this lock.",
 16: "A signature always signs a digest and never the raw transaction, so the rules that build the digest decide what a signature protects. The hash type byte chooses among them, and chapter 8 shows each choice with a measurement. DER matters because two encodings of one signature would give two different byte strings for the same meaning, and nodes must agree on every byte.",
 20: "The m-of-n notation is common: 2-of-3 means any two of three keys, as in a shared account that needs two directors. The oddity of CHECKMULTISIG is a good example of a bug that became part of the rules because fixing it would have split the network. Slide 21 tries the 2-of-3 case.",
 22: "The opcode walks through the keys once and never goes back, so a list of signatures in the wrong order fails even when every signature is valid. The empty dummy rule also removes a way of changing a transaction's identifier without changing its meaning. Both rules are the checks behind the errors on slide 21.",
 24: "P2SH was introduced through BIP 16, and the BIP text is the place to read the exact rules. The idea is a division of labour: the payer needs only a short lock of fixed size, and the spender who wants a complex script pays for its size, so a payer can send to a multisignature wallet without knowing anything about its script.",
 26: "A node works in a fixed order: first the hash check, then the execution. If the revealed script does not hash to the value in the lock, nothing runs at all. Base58Check, which writes the hash as an address, is the same format as in the older addresses that start with 1, only with a different version byte.",
 28: "Block height and Unix time are the two units in which a lock time can be written. Three different tools appear on this slide: OP_RETURN for data, lock time for a whole transaction, and OP_CLTV with relative locks for the coin itself. Keep them apart: a data output can never be spent, whereas the timelocks decide when a spend becomes valid.",
 30: "OP_CLTV is defined in BIP 65, OP_CSV in BIP 112, and BIP 68 explains how the sequence field is read as a relative wait. The size of data that nodes relay in an OP_RETURN output is a policy setting and not a consensus rule. Lock time and CLTV are absolute, sequence and CSV are relative, and each pair belongs together.",
 32: "BIP 68 defines how the sequence field is read as a relative wait, and it is the place to look for the exact bit layout. The VERIFY at the end of both long names means that the script fails at once if the check does not hold, instead of leaving a false value for a later step. Keep the pairs together: lock time with CLTV means a date or a height, sequence with CSV means an age.",
 34: "Every path through an IF branch is a separate way to spend, so more branches mean more cases to test. That is why slide 35 tries every path and every wait, and why a mistake in one branch can leave coins stuck or open to the wrong person.",
 36: "Analysis before use is what makes mistakes in hand-written scripts visible early: a tool can list every way a lock can be spent and what each way costs. Mohammed's capital account from slide 35 is the kind of lock where this helps. The Bitcoin Optech topic page on Miniscript is a good place for further reading. OP_IF, OP_ELSE and OP_ENDIF are the branching words, and OP_VERIFY is the usual way to end a check early. Slide 35 uses them in the account with three paths.",
 38: "Segregated means separated: the witness is kept apart from the rest of the transaction. In a native witness spend the signature and the key move out of the input script into the witness, so the input script itself is empty. The four cards name the key-hash lock, the script-hash lock, and how wallets create and spend them. Slide 39 shows a direct spend and a nested one.",
 41: "The program length is how the new rules tell the two lock kinds apart, and a witness program is recognised by its version number followed by the hash. Wrapping it in P2SH solved a practical problem: wallets that had not yet learned the new addresses could still send coins to it, and the upgrade did not have to wait for them.",
 42: "Because the witness sits apart from the identifier data, changing a signature no longer changes the identifier of a witness spend, which is the removal of a source of malleability. The version number in a witness program leaves room for later upgrades, and taproot later used version 1.",
 43: "BIP 173 defines bech32, and its text is the place for the exact character set and checksum. Addresses that begin with bc1 belong to the segwit family, whereas those that begin with 3 may be old script hashes or nested witness. A soft fork only tightens what was allowed before, which is why old nodes keep accepting the blocks. Slide 41 told how old and new software live side by side, and slide 44 compares the soft fork with a hard fork.",
 46: "The three ideas are the road to taproot: MAST for hiding scripts, P2C for tweaking keys and joint signatures for hiding groups. Each removes information from the chain without removing the guarantee, so spends become smaller and more private. Slide 47 counts the hashes that a proof needs, and slide 48 explains the words root, proof and tweak.",
 48: "Together, the root and the proof let a spender show one script without showing the others. The tweak adds a number derived from a hash to a key, so the key commits to the data without anyone seeing the data. The first two cards make slide 47 work.",
 49: "A group of three that needs two signatures is a typical use. Outsiders see one key and one signature, so a 2-of-3 account looks like any other payment. Simply adding keys has a danger, because one signer can choose a share that cancels the others, and chapter 8 explains what MuSig2 changes.",
 51: "BIP 341 defines the taproot spending rules and BIP 342 defines tapscript. A spender chooses one of two ways: the key path when everybody agrees, and the script path when a particular branch is needed. Slide 52 tests the key path, and slide 55 tests the script path.",
 54: "The tweak ties the output key to the script tree, which is how a script path spend can be checked against it. Anyone can recompute the output key from the internal key and the tree, so nothing has to be taken on trust.",
 56: "Chapter 8 shows how the equation s = k + e x makes this adding-up possible. Mutual satisfaction is the hoped-for case: all parties agree, sign together, and the rules for a dispute never appear on the chain. OP_SUCCESS shows how the language leaves room for upgrades, and slide 58 shows how it works.",
}

EXTRA8 = {
 6: "A digital signature does three things at once: it shows that the key owner approved the message, it ties that approval to this exact message, and it can be checked by anybody. Chapter 7 showed signatures at work inside scripts; this chapter opens the signature itself. Real signatures in Bitcoin are 64 bytes (Schnorr) or about 70 bytes (ECDSA), as slides 24 and 32 measure.",
 8: "Verifying needs only public data, which is why every node can do it. Elliptic curve points are the setting in which the discrete logarithm problem is used in Bitcoin. Remember the pair: signing needs the secret, verifying needs only what is public.",
 12: "In a script, the flag is stored as the last byte of the signature. Choosing a flag means choosing what the signer leaves open: SIGHASH_ALL leaves nothing open, and SIGHASH_NONE leaves every output open. Because the digest is made after the flag has chosen the covered fields, one transaction gives a different digest for each flag. Slide 13 shows how the flag is coded, and slide 14 completes the list.",
 14: "The hash types combine: ANYONECANPAY can be added to ALL, NONE or SINGLE. Taproot's zero byte is only a shorthand, so a default signature carries no flag byte at all and stays at 64 bytes. Slide 15 measures what each flag commits to, and slide 16 shows a bug in SINGLE. The text of BIP118 holds the details of the proposal.",
 18: "The three scriptless ideas share one fact: the chain only ever sees one ordinary key and one ordinary signature. The group structure is kept off the chain, which makes such spends smaller and more private than the scripted multisignature of chapter 7. Slides 19 and 21 build up the equation step by step. A Schnorr signature is made of the point R and the number s, so its size never varies, unlike the DER signatures of ECDSA.",
 20: "Interactive means that a prover talks to a verifier; Fiat-Shamir removes the conversation by letting a hash of the message play the verifier. Keep the order of the ideas in mind: proof, challenge, hash, signature. An x-only key leaves out whether y is even or odd by always assuming an even y, as slide 24 measures in bytes. BIP340 fixes the exact hashing, so wallets from different makers produce signatures that every node can check. Slide 21 shows the Fiat-Shamir step.",
 23: "Linearity makes aggregation possible, batch verification saves work, key prefixing binds the signature to its key and the scriptless form hides the group. Key prefixing is part of the design of BIP340. In the scriptless form the count of signers is hidden inside the keys, and slides 25 and 26 show the danger that comes with it. Compare chapter 7, slide 21, where OP_CHECKMULTISIG counted the signers in a visible script.",
 25: "Shamir secret sharing was published by Adi Shamir in 1979, long before Bitcoin. Two points fix a line, so any two shares recover the secret, while a single share fits every possible line. MuSig2 and Shamir sharing solve different problems: the first protects against a dishonest signer, the second against lost shares. Both ideas let a group act as one key, which is the goal of the scriptless forms on slide 23. Slide 26 shows the attack, and slide 27 shows the sharing.",
 29: "The first release of Bitcoin used ECDSA on the secp256k1 curve, and Schnorr signatures joined it with the taproot upgrade. The inversion in the ECDSA formula is what makes it messier than Schnorr's s = k + e x. Slide 30 follows the algorithm with chosen numbers, and slide 32 takes a real DER signature apart. DER, the byte layout of the pair R and s, is met again on slide 31.",
 31: "Malleability matters because it could change the identifier of a transaction, as chapter 7 explained for spends without a witness. Slide 34 compares consensus with policy. The low-S rule is a relay policy and not a consensus rule, as slide 34 shows, so a wallet that always produces the low form keeps its transactions relayable. The four cards belong together: two forms exist, a relay rule picks one, and DER is how the chosen pair is written down.",
 37: "A weak or repeated nonce has cost real users their funds, as slide 39 tells. RFC 6979 is the cure of slide 40: when nothing random is drawn, there is nothing to repeat. Slide 38 computes the leak, and slide 40 shows the cure. The nonce is the weakest point of the scheme in practice: the mathematics is sound, but a bad random number generator breaks it.",
 43: "BIP143 was written for segregated witness and is not used by legacy outputs. A signature of this kind commits to the amount of the coin that is spent, which is why a changed amount breaks the spend, as chapter 7, slide 39 showed. The old algorithm hashed the transaction again for every input, which is the cost that slide 44 counts.",
}
